---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = "/use-cases/browse-abandonment";
const title = "Shopify browse abandonment without premature offers";
const desc = "Turn identifiable product interest into useful follow-up. Define event quality, product relevance and the handoff to cart recovery before choosing a sender.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "What qualifies someone for a browse reminder?",
    "answer": "Define a usable product-view event, a known eligible recipient and an interest window. A page view alone does not establish permission or purchase intent."
  },
  {
    "question": "When should a browse reminder send?",
    "answer": "Choose timing around the product decision and event freshness. Document what resets or expires interest; there is no universal delay or required number of touches."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should browse emails include discounts?",
    "answer": "Start with the unresolved product question. If proposing an incentive test, approve exclusions and costs and compare it with a reminder-only path rather than assuming past redemption proves need."
  },
  {
    "question": "What happens when browsing becomes a checkout?",
    "answer": "Apply a documented precedence rule so the old browse reminder does not compete with active recovery. Recheck the state before queued sends, and define when suppression expires."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16"><a class="link" href="/use-cases">All use cases</a><h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1><p class="mt-6 text-xl">{desc}</p></section>
  <section class="container pb-16"><article class="card prose-shopify">
<h2>Start with a product question, not an abandoned sale</h2>
<p>A shopper reading material care, fit or compatibility information has not necessarily decided to buy. Write down what the follow-up can help them decide. A useful reminder might link to a size guide or explain a product difference; repeated urgency cannot answer an unresolved specification question. Keep anonymous traffic, identifiable interest and eligible email recipients as separate counts.</p>
<p>Define the event contract with the integration owner: product and variant identifiers, event time, customer key, source page and whether repeated views update existing interest. Do not infer dwell time or view depth if the connector does not supply them. Exclude staff previews and duplicate events from enrollment. Record unavailable signals as limitations rather than substituting invented intent scores.</p>
<h2>Choose which interest survives</h2>
<p>Decide what happens when the same person views several products. The latest view may be a replacement, an accessory or an accidental visit. A proposed policy could group related products into one interest record and expire older categories, but inspect real event semantics before implementing it. Avoid sending a separate reminder for every viewed variant.</p>
<p>Use a consideration window appropriate to the decision. Document timer resets, maximum enrollment duration and re-entry conditions. Do not automatically turn a cold or unopened reminder into a discount branch. If the event arrives late or cannot be joined reliably to a recipient, skip the message and retain the reason for diagnosis.</p>
<h2>Keep recommendations relevant and available</h2>
<p>Show the product name and the attribute the shopper was considering. Recheck availability and the destination before a queued message. If a variant is unavailable, do not silently replace its size or specification. Link to a current product page or offer a separate <a href="/use-cases/back-in-stock">restock signup</a> when appropriate. Avoid implying inventory is reserved.</p>
<p>Separate helpful education from unsupported product advice. For a hypothetical tea catalog, a proposed reminder could explain preparation and link to the exact harvest page. For a hypothetical keyboard catalog, it could link to a compatibility guide while leaving the buyer to confirm their configuration. These are proposed content choices, not merchant results or claims that any vendor generates them correctly.</p>
<h2>Design the handoff to checkout recovery</h2>
<p>Write a state rule for browsing, cart activity, checkout and purchase. Decide which event your system can actually observe. A proposed policy might pause browse follow-up once checkout recovery starts, then cancel it after a matched purchase. Recheck that rule before sending, including messages already waiting in a queue. Missing identity matches remain a known limitation.</p>
<p>A pause needs an expiry and release condition. Otherwise an old checkout can suppress future interest indefinitely. Review welcome messages and editorial campaigns separately: an educational newsletter may still be useful, while a simultaneous competing offer may need suppression. Use <a href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">cart recovery</a> for checkout-specific contract details and <a href="/guides/flow-collision-prevention">collision prevention</a> for journey ownership.</p>
<h2>Decide whether an offer answers the problem</h2>
<p>Compare a product explanation, a reminder and an approved incentive as different interventions. Define product exclusions, stacking rules, expiry and margin ownership before an offer goes live. Prior redemption shows that a code was used; it does not establish that the customer would refuse full price. Do not force every recipient through a prescribed three-message escalation.</p>
<p>Evaluate eligible recipient cohorts over comparable observation windows. Separate sends, clicks, carts, matched orders, refunds and incentive cost. Platform-attributed orders are not proof of incremental purchases. If a controlled comparison is feasible, specify assignment and purchase measurement before starting. See <a href="/use-cases/analytics">analytics</a> for reporting boundaries.</p>
<h2>Proposed acceptance checks</h2>
<p>These checks have not been executed here. Create controlled cases for duplicate views, multiple categories, a sold-out variant, an event arriving late, a checkout created during the wait, a purchase before send and a recipient becoming ineligible. Inspect the enrollment record, chosen product, queued-message exit and final destination. Check that a second device or session does not create unexplained duplicate reminders.</p>
<p>For each case, retain the event identifier, expected decision, actual decision and owner of any mismatch. A delivered test message only demonstrates that particular rendering path; it does not prove eligibility, freshness or purchase suppression. Require the operator to explain skipped messages as well as successful enrollment.</p>
<h2>Compare tools against this browse contract</h2>
<p>Use the <a href="/apps">tool catalog</a> to shortlist Sequenzy and other candidates. Ask each to demonstrate the exact product event, identity join, repeat-view handling, variant lookup, queued exits and inspection logs in the intended plan. Record what is native, what needs another integration and what requires custom work. This page does not rank vendors or certify current features.</p>
<p>Prefer a system whose operator can reconstruct why a recipient received one product rather than another. Keep the interest-window policy and handoff rules outside the workflow canvas so a replacement operator can review them. Revisit the policy when catalog structure or tracking changes, rather than tightening arbitrary thresholds on a calendar.</p>
  </article></section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
</BaseLayout>
